<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chalcis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chalkida</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chalcis is the chief town of the Greek island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>, on the narrow Euripus strait facing the mainland. In antiquity it was a major city that founded many colonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, as Negroponte, it was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> triarchs and of Venetian rule from the 13th century until the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> took it in 1470.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chalcis.jpg" alt="Chalcis" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: C messier · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%A7%CE%B1%CE%BB%CE%BA%CE%AF%CE%B4%CE%B1_0067.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Garrisons at Corinth, <b>Chalcis</b> and Demetrias, which Philip V reportedly called the ‘fetters of Greece’, anchored Macedonian control of the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Thucydides records that the Corinthian shipwright Ameinocles built four ships for the Samians around 700 BCE, and in the Lelantine War the island is said to have sided with <b>Chalcis</b> while its rival Miletus supported Eretria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/samos">Samos · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Sultan Mehmed II personally led the Ottoman siege of Negroponte (<b>Chalcis</b>), capital of the Venetian possession of Euboea.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-12">12 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It lost its independence after the Achaean War of 146 BCE, when <b>Chalcis</b> was punished for supporting the Achaeans.</blockquote><a href="/state/euboean-league">Euboean League</a></li></ul></section>
